    "title" : "CorePathWrangler"
  },
  "primaryContentSections" : [
    {
      "content" : [
        {
          "anchor" : "Installation",
          "level" : 2,
          "text" : "Installation",
          "type" : "heading"
        },
        {
          "inlineContent" : [
            {
              "text" : "Add the following package dependency in your ",
              "type" : "text"
            },
            {
              "code" : "Package.swift",
              "type" : "codeVoice"
            },
            {
              "text" : ":",
              "type" : "text"
            }
          ],
          "type" : "paragraph"
        },
        {
          "code" : [
            ".package(url: \"https:\/\/github.com\/sersoft-gmbh\/path-wrangler\", from: \"2.0.0\"),"
          ],
          "syntax" : "swift",
          "type" : "codeListing"
        },
        {
          "inlineContent" : [
            {
              "text" : "Or add it via Xcode (as of Xcode 11).",
              "type" : "text"
            }
          ],
          "type" : "paragraph"
        },
        {
          "anchor" : "Usage",
          "level" : 2,
          "text" : "Usage",
          "type" : "heading"
        },
        {
          "inlineContent" : [
            {
              "text" : "PathWrangler has two basic representations of a path: ",
              "type" : "text"
            },
            {
              "identifier" : "doc:\/\/CorePathWrangler\/documentation\/CorePathWrangler\/AbsolutePath",
              "isActive" : true,
              "type" : "reference"
            },
            {
              "text" : " and ",
              "type" : "text"
            },
            {
              "identifier" : "doc:\/\/CorePathWrangler\/documentation\/CorePathWrangler\/RelativePath",
              "isActive" : true,
              "type" : "reference"
            },
            {
              "text" : ". They have everything you need for simple yet great path computations.",
              "type" : "text"
            },
            {
              "text" : " ",
              "type" : "text"
            },
            {
              "text" : "A relative path (as the name suggests) is not bound to any root. It’s relative to whatever absolute path you need it to be.",
              "type" : "text"
            },
            {
              "text" : " ",
              "type" : "text"
            },
            {
              "text" : "Thus it can also be turned into an absolute path using ",
              "type" : "text"
            },
            {
              "identifier" : "doc:\/\/CorePathWrangler\/documentation\/CorePathWrangler\/RelativePath\/absolute(in:)",
              "isActive" : true,
              "type" : "reference"
            },
            {
              "text" : ".",
              "type" : "text"
            },
            {
              "text" : " ",
              "type" : "text"
            },
            {
              "text" : "An absolute path on the other hand is a path that always starts at the root.",
              "type" : "text"
            }
          ],
          "type" : "paragraph"
        },
        {
          "inlineContent" : [
            {
              "text" : "Both paths know how to “resolve” (or “simplify”) themselves. By doing so, they try to resolve and thus remove references of the current folder (",
              "type" : "text"
            },
            {
              "code" : ".",
              "type" : "codeVoice"
            },
            {
              "text" : ") and parent folder (",
              "type" : "text"
            },
            {
              "code" : "..",
              "type" : "codeVoice"
            },
            {
              "text" : ").",
              "type" : "text"
            },
            {
              "text" : " ",
              "type" : "text"
            },
            {
              "text" : "Since absolute paths know where to start, they are even able to resolve symlinks. To do so, however, the path must exist on disk.",
              "type" : "text"
            },
            {
              "text" : " ",
              "type" : "text"
            },
            {
              "text" : "Both path implementations also have a ",
              "type" : "text"
            },
            {
              "code" : "current",
              "type" : "codeVoice"
            },
            {
              "text" : " accessor that returns the current path.",
              "type" : "text"
            },
            {
              "text" : " ",
              "type" : "text"
            },
            {
              "text" : "For ",
              "type" : "text"
            },
            {
              "identifier" : "doc:\/\/CorePathWrangler\/documentation\/CorePathWrangler\/AbsolutePath",
              "isActive" : true,
              "type" : "reference"
            },
            {
              "text" : " this is the ",
              "type" : "text"
            },
            {
              "code" : "cwd",
              "type" : "codeVoice"
            },
            {
              "text" : ". For ",
              "type" : "text"
            },
            {
              "identifier" : "doc:\/\/CorePathWrangler\/documentation\/CorePathWrangler\/RelativePath",
              "isActive" : true,
              "type" : "reference"
            },
            {
              "text" : " this is always just the relative path to the current directory, thus ",
              "type" : "text"
            },
            {
              "code" : ".",
              "type" : "codeVoice"
            },
            {
              "text" : ".",
              "type" : "text"
            }
          ],
          "type" : "paragraph"
        },
        {
          "inlineContent" : [
            {
              "text" : "Path components or a relative path can be appended to both paths. There is even a convenience API that allows path building that almost looks like the path representation:",
              "type" : "text"
            }
          ],
          "type" : "paragraph"
        },
        {
          "code" : [
            "let someSubDir = AbsolutePath.root \/ \"folder1\" \/ \"folder2\" \/\/ -> \"\/folder1\/folder2\""
          ],
          "syntax" : "swift",
          "type" : "codeListing"
        },
        {
          "inlineContent" : [
            {
              "text" : "A protocol named ",
              "type" : "text"
            },
            {
              "identifier" : "doc:\/\/CorePathWrangler\/documentation\/CorePathWrangler\/PathComponentConvertible",
              "isActive" : true,
              "type" : "reference"
            },
            {
              "text" : " is used to represent path components. A bunch of default conformances (like for ",
              "type" : "text"
            },
            {
              "code" : "String",
              "type" : "codeVoice"
            },
            {
              "text" : ", ",
              "type" : "text"
            },
            {
              "code" : "Int",
              "type" : "codeVoice"
            },
            {
              "text" : ", etc.) make working with it easy.",
              "type" : "text"
            },
            {
              "text" : " ",
              "type" : "text"
            },
            {
              "text" : "But adding more conformances is also easy. Just implement the only requirement ",
              "type" : "text"
            },
            {
              "code" : "pathComponent",
              "type" : "codeVoice"
            },
            {
              "text" : " and return the path component of the conforming type.",
              "type" : "text"
            },
            {
              "text" : " ",
              "type" : "text"
            },
            {
              "text" : "As of then, the type can be used wherever path components are used in PathWrangler (like with the nice ",
              "type" : "text"
            },
            {
              "code" : "\/",
              "type" : "codeVoice"
            },
            {
              "text" : " API mentioned before).",
              "type" : "text"
            }
          ],
          "type" : "paragraph"
        }
      ],
      "kind" : "content"
    }
  ],
